WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.120
What’s up Wisecrack?

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.130
Michael here.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.490
Now I’m sure a lot of you are sick of hearing
about the Oscars.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.070
You either don’t care and are starting to
wonder why you clicked this video, or you

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.570
do care, and are deeply offended by the following
statement: Nobody should care about the Academy

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.570
Awards.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.820
We say this as film lovers who have been burned
one-too-many times by the Academy’s bizarre

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.930
choices, and we say it as philosophy students
trying to understand how Crash could possibly

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.449
be considered the most significant film of
2004.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:34.629
“You want something from me?

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:36.379
Cause I’m right here.

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:41.350
I’m trying to help you.I didn’t ask for
your help did?”

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.210
But most of all, we say it as human beings
who hate the media discourse surrounding the

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.319
Oscars so much we considered entering a sensory
deprivation tank for the duration of this

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:51.319
year’s award season.

00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:53.219
Jokes aside - what’s the big deal?

00:00:53.219 --> 00:01:03.379
What’s wrong with people celebrating how
much they love Green Book?

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:08.540
The way we see it, the problem lies in the
profound influence the Academy Awards have

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.210
on the creation of new movies.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.360
But we’ll get there.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.610
So without further ado,

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.300
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition: The Oscars
Were  a Mistake

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.350
Every year, tens of millions of people tune
into to see who will receive the film industry’s

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:28.350
highest honors.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.120
But the Academy that doles out Oscars wasn’t
just created to celebrate performances by

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.380
actors like Nicolas Cage in “Leaving Las
Vegas.”

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.380
Ah!

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.380
Ah!

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:39.380
Ahh!

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:45.960
It was instead created to uh, pay people in
the film industry less.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.450
The year is 1926, and you’re Louis B. Mayer,
a MGM studio mogul preparing to do the most

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.400
studio mogul-ly-thing of all: build a fancy
Santa Monica beach house.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.570
As recounted by critic and author David Thompson
in Vanity Fair, Mayer wanted to hire some

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:03.209
MGM set designers to help construct his oceanside
paradise.

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:07.380
But there was a problem: the set designers
were just about to become part of Hollywood’s

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.020
first union, complete with new contracts that
made them very expensive.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.270
In case you were worried about the well-being
of Mayer’s new home, he managed by hiring

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.370
a few pricey set designers and relying on
cheaper laborers for the rest.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.770
But the dude was more or less haunted by the
idea that actors, directors and screenwriters

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.370
would follow suit and unionize for better
wages.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.660
So, he concocted a genius plan: he made his
own union.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.099
He beat them to the punch, creating an institution
that could swoop up his opposition and stop

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:37.180
unions before they even started.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.970
It was the historical equivalent of your boss
giving you a giftcard to your local TGI Fridays

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.130
instead of that raise you asked for.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.239
Mayer’s new institution was the Academy
of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences - The

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:49.600
Academy, for short.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.140
If you think the long name sounds snooty,
that’s the whole point, according to Thomson,

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:58.800
“It made you think the Academy had always
been there, arranged by God and Harvard and and

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.660
Albert Einstein” and not a few cigar-smoking
jerks looking for cheap non-union labor.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.950
At its inception, the Academy was comprised
of 36 actors, directors, writers, technicians

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:08.950
and producers.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.910
They were conferred elite status and the vague
promise that working conditions in the industry

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.140
would “improve.”

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.209
But while the Academy promised to create industry
standards and handle labor disputes, it was

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:21.340
privately viewed as a way to render unions
unnecessary.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.810
On a broader level, the organization was dedicated
to promoting a positive, wholesome image of

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:30.780
Hollywood during a decade plagued by industry
scandals in the form of Charlie Chaplin’s

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.590
scandalous divorce or Fatty Arbuckle being
tried for drunken manslaughter.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:40.240
Anyway, these powerful, image-conscious folks
had a fancy banquet in January of 1927.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.220
The only thing the festivities were missing?

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.730
Something to make people feel even MORE special,
a “legitimizing” factor, if you will.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:48.830
Enter: awards.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:53.080
As Mayer recounted to his biographer, “I
found that the best way to handle [movie makers]

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.760
was to hang medals all over them.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.199
If I got them cups and awards, they’d kill
themselves to produce what I wanted.

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:02.210
That’s why the Academy Award was created.”

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.050
Come 1929, the first awards were handed out
in a 15 minute private ceremony.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.240
These awards were also a way for the movie
industry to affirm its own artistic importance.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.240
On one level, this can be seen as obvious
self-indulgence.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.930
On another level, scholar Peter Decherney
argues, it was a way to brand Hollywood talent

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:25.169
as “skilled artistry” rather than “labor,”
in the process, rendering labor unions unnecessary.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:29.729
Decherney argues that the award categories
themselves created two separate classes of

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:33.110
Hollywood workers - lowly laborers and “artistes.”

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:37.740
However, Mayer’s ingenious solution to the
pesky union problem was ultimately doomed.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:43.199
The economic turmoil of the early 1930s led
everyone from actors to writers to directors

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:44.199
to organize.

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:48.639
In a delicious twist, fledgling unions like
the Screen Actors’ Guild, the Writers’

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:52.880
Guild and the Directors’ Guild boycotted
subsequent Oscar ceremonies in successful

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.850
attempts to strongarm the Academy into negotiations.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:58.680
Those unions remain powerful to this day.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.710
Still, that annual love letter to Hollywood’s
most glamorous residents would become one

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:07.320
of the most effective promotional tools of
all time establishing American film as the

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.509
world’s primary form of entertainment.

00:06:09.509 --> 00:06:13.901
Increasingly fawning media coverage intensified
America’s emotional investment in the movie

00:06:13.901 --> 00:06:17.790
industry and especially, in the celebrities
who win and lose shiny prizes.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:22.169
As the cult of celebrity grew, obsessive interest
in the fashions worn, the attractive dates

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:25.259
escorted, and the scandalous afterparties
ballooned.

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:28.199
So now we know the shady origins of the Oscars.

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:31.979
But the real question is --Are they even good
at what they do?

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:36.100
After all, if your goal is to award artistic
excellence, you’d hope said excellence would

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.630
withstand the test of time.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:42.440
Or at least do so better than, say the 1956
Best Picture Around the World in 80 Days or

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.420
the 1989 gem Driving Miss Daisy.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.090
While an Oscar award is pretty ubiquitously
seen as a confirmation of artistic merit,

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.949
some decisions the Academy has made have cast
doubt on the industry’s ability to appraise

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:53.949
its own work.

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:55.360
And that’s putting it politely.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.000
“This seemed like a better idea in rehearsal”

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:04.350
Last year alone, controversy brewed over choices
like Best Editing for Bohemian Rhapsody, a

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:09.070
film which IndieWire called “a masterclass
in bad editing” and which even the editor

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:14.490
HIMSELF admits of one scene: “Whenever I
see it, I want to put a bag over my head.”

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.960
Then there was the Best Picture for Peter
Farrelly’s Green Book, which LA Times critic

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.860
Justin Chang succinctly called “an embarrassment.”

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.520
The matter of taste has haunted the Oscars
from the very beginning, with the industry

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:29.699
perpetually agonizing over its desire to advance
the art of filmmaking and to spotlight commercial

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:30.699
successes.

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:33.660
We’re going to focus on the history of the
Best Picture, by the way, because it can help

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.470
succinctly shine light on larger issues in
the academy.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.060
So apologies to you best costume design historians.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.350
In its first year, the Academy straddled this
line by awarding two best pictures.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.130
One went to the most “Outstanding Picture,”
the commercially successful World War I film

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:51.860
Wings and one to the most “Unique and Artistic
Picture” the expressionist film Sunrise:

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.510
A Song of Two Humans.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.760
It created a division between mainstream and
“artistic” excellence.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:01.139
But then, the Academy tweaked things - creating
one “ultimate” best picture category.

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:06.210
Since then, that award has represented a tough
balancing act between entertainment value,

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.630
artisanship, commercial success, and the film
industry’s public image.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:14.840
All of which must be reconciled when Hollywood
endorses one film as the definitive “best.”

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.810
This has resulted in many questionable choices.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:23.220
In 1953, the still-widely-respected film Singin
in the Rain wasn’t even considered for Best

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.220
Picture.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.520
The award instead went to The Greatest Show
on Earth which has an abysmal 43% critic score

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:29.660
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:34.039
The wildly inventive film Brazil - a Wisecrack
favorite, so maybe we’re a little biased

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.930
- also didn’t manage to get nominated for
best picture in 1986.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:40.270
Instead, the top prize went to Out of Africa.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:46.021
Brazil has a 98% critic approval on Rotten
Tomatoes, and a 90% audience approval - speaking

00:08:46.021 --> 00:08:48.890
to its long-lasting appeal and artistic brilliance.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:54.200
Meanwhile, Out of Africa has a 58% critic
score and a not-so-bad 83% audience score.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.390
What’s more, Gladiator won best picture
in 2000 when the god-damn freaking MATRIX

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.940
wasn’t even nominated.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.230
I’m going to stop this list before I burst
a blood vessel, but I think the question has

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:09.800
been raised: Why has the Academy proven to
be both predictable and frankly annoying in

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.850
its choice of best picture nominees and winners?

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.260
There’s a certain pattern when it comes
to the Oscar’s highest prize.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:19.410
Most notably, genre makes a big difference
for Best Picture - Dramas tend to rack up

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:24.700
the top prize, winning 89% of the time between
1928 and 2010, according to Collider.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.910
Specific types of drama also have an easy
way in: A UCLA study of Best Picture nominees

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:35.250
between 1985 and 2009 found that war movies,
historical epics and biographies fare incredibly

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:36.250
well.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.230
Meanwhile, IMDB keywords like “family tragedy,”
“whistleblower,” “domestic servant,”

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.720
and “Watergate” correlated most highly
with nominations.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:48.320
Collider also noted that the Oscar tends to
go to the longest film nominated and that

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:54.470
80% of Best Picture winners since 1980 clocked
in at over two hours.American Film History

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:59.010
Damiel Bona sums up the vibe of Best Picture
winners as: “Movies that are aesthetically

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.540
conservative, but which contain humanistic
or uplifting attitudes.”

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.970
That is movies that make Hollywood feel important
WITHOUT challenging the status quo - which

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:10.589
is why heartwarming romantic war dramas like
The English Patient beat arguably-better,

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:13.080
but decidedly-nihilistic films like Fargo.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.940
So what, in contrast, DOESN’T win Best Picture?

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:20.420
Negatively associated are IMDB terms like
“zombie” and “black independent film.”

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:24.690
Which is why the objectively groundbreaking,
hasn’t-aged-a-day Night of the Living Dead

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:29.220
lost to the decidedly-dated musical Oliver,
and also why Spike Lee didn’t win an Oscar

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:34.269
until 30 years after the release of Do The
Right Thing which lost to, and I can’t stress

00:10:34.269 --> 00:10:36.640
this enough, Driving Miss Daisy.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.750
“Mornin Ms. Daisy I thought I’d see after
your

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:41.750
Zinnias”.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:42.750
“You leave my flower bed alone”.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.080
Oh, and if you’re making a kickass fantasy
film, or horror film, don’t hold your breath

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.509
waiting for a nomination.

00:10:47.509 --> 00:10:52.400
After all, even Psycho, almost universally
regarded as the best horror film of all time,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:54.440
wasn’t nominated for Best Picture.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.860
That was the same year flicks like Elmer Gantry,
Sons and Lovers, and The Sundowners, none

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.649
of which you’ve heard of, were nominated.

00:11:00.649 --> 00:11:04.640
Not even one of the finest works of sci-fi
is likely to earn a nomination, including

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.180
the famously-snubbed, breathtaking 2001: A
Space Odyssey.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.440
In fact, a sci-fi film has never won Best
Picture.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:15.270
It might sound like we’re harping about
all our favorite films that didn’t snag

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.990
best picture nominations - but it’s not
just that - the list of films not given Oscar

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:25.050
nods wouldn’t be so objectionable if only
so many TERRIBLE or forgettable films weren’t

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.050
lauded.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.240
Somewhat humorously, the status-quo obsessed
nature of the Oscars can perhaps best be illustrated

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:32.170
by a decision made in 1929.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:37.089
The very first feature-length talking picture
was disqualified for consideration because

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:39.620
it had an ``unfair” advantage over silent
films.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:44.649
The tendency for the Academy to reward “safe”
choices is only augmented by their preferential

00:11:44.649 --> 00:11:47.760
voting system, in which voters rank nominees
in order of preference.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.490
As a result, films that are “less disliked”
tend to triumph over films that are more polarizing,

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:53.670
innovative or “risky.”

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:58.740
The industry's desire to simultaneously seem
important and recognize commercially successful

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:03.160
films means that it tends to nominate inspiring
films based around popular historical events

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.430
and people (cough Lincoln) - even when those
stories skew the facts about historical figures

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:08.430
or events.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:12.610
What’s more, films featuring actors who
“transform” physically into familiar characters

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:16.770
from history or literature are also frequently
rewarded, whether they’re playing Freddie

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:19.730
Mercury or Winston Churchill or Queen Elizabeth.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:23.990
Above all though, naturally, your film must
not ruffle the feathers of the Academy, which

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:29.271
is why beloved, provocative films like American
Psycho or Tangerine tend not to not attract

00:12:29.271 --> 00:12:33.899
the same attention as safe, non-paradigm shifting
films like The King’s Speech or Frost/Nixon.

00:12:33.899 --> 00:12:37.870
It also must not be seen as quote unquote
“cheap” commercial fare like Back to the

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:38.870
Future.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:42.770
And, we’d be remiss NOT to point out that
a “foreign-language film” has NEVER won

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.930
best picture, though we are rooting HARD for
Parasite to change that.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:49.339
In recent decades, Hollywood has attempted
to make itself more relevant by tweaking the

00:12:49.339 --> 00:12:51.740
awards to recognize more popular films [Joker].

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.140
They introduced a Best Animated Film category
in 2002 and expanded the Best Pictures category

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.110
from five to ten nominees after The Dark Knight
was infamously snubbed.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:05.050
Most notably, the Academy proposed reverting
back to its original, 1929 paradigm for the

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:09.269
2019 Oscars by introducing an award for “outstanding
achievement in popular film.”

00:13:09.269 --> 00:13:13.810
This announcement, ironically, was quickly
met with outrage at the Academy’s apparent

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:18.269
insinuation that a highly-commercially successful
film couldn’t ALSO be the best film of the

00:13:18.269 --> 00:13:19.269
year.

00:13:19.269 --> 00:13:22.279
They quickly rescinded the decision before
promptly, and predictably, giving its highest

00:13:22.279 --> 00:13:28.050
honors to a 130-minute historically-inaccurate
drama featuring an uplifting interracial friendship

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:30.450
during American segregation.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:33.860
Speaking of Green Book,“important” and
“popular” films that tend to win also

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.190
seem to skew preachy,.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:41.190
“So that little temper tantrum, was it worth
it?

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.310
Hm?

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:47.760
You never win with violence Tony, you only
win when you maintain your dignity, dignity

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.300
always prevails”.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.450
Without asking too many hard questions or
being too provocative.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:56.980
So we get a lot of feel-good interracial friendships,
and a lot less horse-people-fueled revolutions.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:01.800
Which gets us to Oscar bait - a catch-all
term for the way studios try to game their

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:05.800
way into a Best Picture nomination - whether
that be the kind of film they make, the time

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.130
they release the film, or the marketing strategy
surrounding it.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:13.970
Oscar bait can only exist *because* the Oscars
are so predictable, for all the previously

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:15.250
cited reasons.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:19.709
This mutually reinforcing system means that
studios KEEP making more English Patients

00:14:19.709 --> 00:14:23.790
and War Horses and timing them to coincide
with the fall “Oscar season.”

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:28.780
While Oscar bait existed as early as the 1930s,
it really came into its dismal own when the

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:30.640
studio system was broken up.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:34.980
This meant that film studios no longer could
control movie theaters, meaning they had to

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.670
more strategically plot to snag their movies
impressive theatrical runs.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.930
Arguably the biggest breakthrough in Oscar
marketing came in 1978 with the late December

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:45.700
release of Deer Hunter.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:50.860
The film is great, but Universal Studios determined
that it was too intense and sad to organically

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:55.360
become a box office success, so they planned
its entire marketing strategy around an Oscar

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:56.360
nomination.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:01.470
Spoiler alert: the strategy worked and Deer
Hunter snagged 9 nominations, and 5 wins including

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:02.470
Best Picture.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.410
In the process, it changed the way studios
thought about the awards.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:10.140
And so every fall, we get a predictable parade
of movies about a world war, or a conflicted

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:14.329
monarch, or an inspiring character overcoming
the odds to succeed because that’s the gosh-darned

00:15:14.329 --> 00:15:15.329
American way.

00:15:15.329 --> 00:15:18.790
And yes - exceptions exist, but it’s an
undeniable trend.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:23.139
Along with the rise of the Oscar bait has
come the intensification of pre-Oscar marketing

00:15:23.139 --> 00:15:24.139
campaigns.

00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:28.360
Now, swaying Academy judges with more than
just talent is as old as the Oscars themselves.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:32.480
When the awards were just a year old, best
actress nominee, Mary Pickford had the entire

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.830
voting committee over for tea at her estate,
an invitation then considered Tinseltown’s

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:37.830
greatest status symbol.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.139
She subsequently won her Best Actress award,
but could victory really have felt that great?

00:15:42.139 --> 00:15:45.899
Advertising campaigns soon extended beyond
tea parties, with “For Your consideration”

00:15:45.899 --> 00:15:49.010
ads debuting in newspapers in 1936.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:55.550
By 1955, the film Marty boasted an Oscar budget
of $400,000 that was higher than its entire

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:57.740
production cost of $343,000.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:01.730
For this effort, the film won four little
gold men.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:05.529
As we entered the new millenium, marketing
had "escalated to unprecedented degrees,”

00:16:05.529 --> 00:16:06.550
as Bona notes.

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:10.730
All of which is to say, independent films
like “Sorry to Bother You” simply can’t

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:12.700
compete, as Boots Riley explained on Twitter.

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:16.240
That’s even if, based on merit, those films
are just as excellent.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.660
And the films that DO win nominations or Oscars
get incomparable boosts in ticket sales and

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.990
name brand recognition, even if they’re
called Crash.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:28.149
So what is the mindless production, promotion,
and consumption of Oscar-bait films, doing

00:16:28.149 --> 00:16:29.149
to the industry?

00:16:29.149 --> 00:16:33.600
We’d argue that, over the past half-century,
it led to a great many talented people producing

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:38.579
a great many NOT awesome films, with the conscious
or subconscious goal of winning a little gold

00:16:38.579 --> 00:16:39.579
man.

00:16:39.579 --> 00:16:43.459
Those studio-backed films then suck up nearly
all available press attention What that inevitably

00:16:43.459 --> 00:16:47.850
means for a population with only SO much time
and money to spend at the movie theater is:

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:52.240
Less attention and less acclaim for genuinely
GOOD and provocative films that don’t conform

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.950
to stodgy Oscar standards.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.920
When you look through the history of films
that have truly made the medium what it is

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:01.079
today, you don’t find a filmography of Oscar
Best Picture winners - you find films that

00:17:01.079 --> 00:17:02.839
were too daring to pander.

00:17:02.839 --> 00:17:06.919
All of which is to say, skip the Oscars this
year and watch a couple of movies that aren’t

00:17:06.919 --> 00:17:08.329
afraid to alienate you.

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:12.909
Try Brazil or Spring Breakers, Advantageous
or Personal Shopper, The Witch or Under the

00:17:12.909 --> 00:17:13.929
Silver Lake.

00:17:13.929 --> 00:17:15.799
Honestly, anything but Green Book.

00:17:15.799 --> 00:17:17.359
But hey - what do you think?

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:20.980
Thanks to all our patrons for supporting our
podcast and the channel.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.339
Click that subscribe button, or you’re part
of the problem.

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:31.419
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:17:31.419 --> 00:17:39.409
Later.

